data.content:
Here's what changed:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    // Parameterless ctor keeps existing callers working — no DI required
    public InvoiceCalculator() : this(TimeProvider.System) { }

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => timeProvider.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

### 2. In tests, use `FakeTimeProvider`

Install the test helper once:
```
dotnet add <TestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

Then:
```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 3, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 28, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 28, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

### Why this is the lightest option

- **No custom interface or wrapper** — `TimeProvider` is built into .NET 8+.
- **No DI container needed** — the parameterless constructor defaults to real time, so existing callers don't change.
- **No ambient/static state** — each test controls its own instance.

If you're on .NET 6/7, install `Microsoft.Bcl.TimeProvider` — same API, just backported.
